\f0\b \cf0 \ul ## Project Overview
\f1\b0 \ulnone \
\
This research addresses a central challenge in social\'96ecological systems research by developing a process-based approach to model wildlife-accessible food waste in urban environments. We conceptualize food waste production as a socio-political-ecological process, integrating urban ecology with political ecology theory to move beyond coarse, static social proxies that traditionally limit inference for socially-produced ecological variables.\
\
Our mixed-methods approach combines field observations, bird foraging data, sociodemographic information, land-use patterns, and waste infrastructure data from Vancouver, Canada. Using a generative Bayesian hierarchical model, we predict food waste distribution across 750 m\'b2 grid cells, revealing how housing markets, infrastructure, and governance structure urban ecological resource subsidies.\
\
The project identifies two key latent socio-political factors\'97Housing Tenure\'96Density Scale and Enfranchisement Scale\'97that strongly predict waste availability, showing concentration in dense, renter-dominated, economically marginalized areas near food retailers, with minimal waste in affluent, low-density neighborhoods. This work provides a replicable, low-cost framework for explicitly representing social processes in social\'96ecological models, advancing quantitative and theoretical understanding of coupled human-environment systems.\
\

\f0\b \ul ## Research Question(s)
\f1\b0 \ulnone \
\
- (1) Do socio-demographic, infrastructural, and land-use variables predict the distribution and magnitude of wildlife-accessible food waste?\
- (2) Does explicitly representing socio-political processes in relationship with ecological processes  improve model performance and interpretability?\
- Hypothesis: We expected socio-economic and infrastructural gradients (represented by data on income, housing tenure and density, and road ways) \
to shape the distribution of wildlife-accessible food waste, reflecting differences in waste service provision and the divergent everyday experiences of people in different socio-economic classes. \
\

